var definitions = ['<h2>Tu peux compter sur moi!</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when addressing one person in an informal context. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>peux</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "pouvoir", which means "to be able to" or "can". It denotes the ability or capacity of the subject.</div><div><strong>compter</strong> This is an infinitive verb in French, meaning "to count". In this sentence, it’s used after the helper verb "peux", together creating the meaning "can count on".</div><div><strong>sur</strong> This is a preposition in French that means "on" in English. It is used in this sentence to indicate reliance or dependence on someone or something.</div><div><strong>moi</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "me" in English. It’s used here as the object of the preposition "sur", conveying the meaning "on me".</div><div>The entire sentence <em>Tu peux compter sur moi!</em> translates to "You can count on me!" in English, conveying a message of reassurance and support.</div>','<h2>Embrasse-moi!</h2><div><strong>Embrasse</strong> This is the imperative form of the French verb "embrasser", which means "to kiss". In this command form, it’s directed to "you" (the person being spoken to), telling them to perform the action.</div><div><strong>-moi</strong> This is a direct object pronoun in the imperative form, attached to the verb "embrasse" with a hyphen. "Moi" means "me" in English, so it indicates that the speaker is asking to be kissed by the listener.</div>','<h2>Aide-moi à monter, s’il te plaît.</h2><div><strong>Aide-moi</strong> This is an imperative form of the verb "aider", which means "to help". "Aide" is the imperative for "tu" (informal you), and "moi" is the object pronoun "me". So together, "Aide-moi" means "Help me".</div><div><strong>à</strong> This preposition is used here with the verb "monter" to create the meaning "to climb or to go up". In this context, it forms part of the verbal phrase "à monter".</div><div><strong>monter</strong> This is an infinitive verb that means "to climb" or "to go up". In the sentence, it’s used after "à", which indicates the action that help is being requested for.</div><div><strong>s’il te plaît</strong> This is a polite expression that translates to "please". Literally, "s’il te plaît" means "if it pleases you". "S’il" is a contraction of "si" (if) + "il" (it), "te" is the second-person singular object pronoun (to you), and "plaît" is the third-person singular present of the verb "plaire" (to please).</div>','<h2>Écoute-moi quand je te parle!</h2><div><strong>Écoute-moi</strong> This phrase is a command in French, which means "Listen to me". "Écoute" is the imperative form of the verb "écouter", which means "to listen". The "-moi" is a direct object pronoun, which means "me". All together, it tells someone directly to pay attention to what the speaker is saying.</div><div><strong>quand</strong> This is a French conjunction that means "when". It is used to introduce a dependent clause.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the dependent clause.</div><div><strong>te</strong> This is the second-person singular object pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. In this sentence, it is an indirect object pronoun used before the verb "parle" to whom the speech is directed.</div><div><strong>parle</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "parler", which means "to speak" or "to talk". Here, it indicates the action that the speaker is performing while asking to be listened to.</div>','<h2>J’aime danser avec toi.</h2><div><strong>J’</strong> This is the contraction of "Je", the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English, when followed by a vowel sound. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>aime</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "aimer", which means "to love" or "to like". In this sentence, it expresses the speaker’s enjoyment or love for the action that follows.</div><div><strong>danser</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "danser", which means "to dance". It is the action that the speaker enjoys doing.</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This preposition means "with" in English. It is used to indicate accompaniment or togetherness in an action.</div><div><strong>toi</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It’s the object of the preposition "avec" and specifies with whom the speaker enjoys dancing.</div>','<h2>Tous ces cadeaux sont pour toi.</h2><div><strong>Tous</strong> This is the French word for "all". It is used here to refer to every one of a group of things, in this case, the gifts.</div><div><strong>ces</strong> This is the word for "these" in French. It is a demonstrative adjective used to specify which gifts are being talked about.</div><div><strong>cadeaux</strong> This is the French word for "gifts". It is in the plural form to match "tous" and "ces", referring to several gifts.</div><div><strong>sont</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be" in English. It links the subject "cadeaux" to the complement of the sentence.</div><div><strong>sont pour</strong> When combined, this phrase translates to "are for" in English, indicating the intended recipient of the gifts.</div><div><strong>toi</strong> This is the French word for "you" when addressing a single person in an informal setting. It serves as an indirect object pronoun in this sentence, receiving the action.</div>','<h2>Toi et moi, pour toujours!</h2><div><strong>Toi</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when used as an object or for emphasis. In this phrase, it is used for emphasis and means "you" specifically, addressing one person intimately or informally.</div><div><strong>et</strong> This word translates to "and" in English. It’s a conjunction used to connect words or groups of words in both languages.</div><div><strong>moi</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "me" in English. It is used here in a stressed form, emphasizing the speaker themselves.</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This is a preposition in French that translates to "for" in English. It’s used to indicate the duration or purpose of something.</div><div><strong>toujours</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "forever" or "always" in English. It’s used to signify an indefinite, unending duration of time.</div>','<h2>J’aimerais être grand comme toi.</h2><div><strong>J’aimerais</strong> This is the conditional form of "aimer", which means "to like" or "to love" in French. Here, it indicates a desire or wish, translating to "I would like" in English.</div><div><strong>être</strong> This is the infinitive of the verb "être", which means "to be" in English. In this sentence, it’s used after "aimerais" to express what the subject would like to be.</div><div><strong>grand</strong> This is an adjective meaning "tall" or "big" in English. It describes a characteristic the speaker wishes to have.</div><div><strong>comme</strong> This is a French preposition that means "like" or "as" in English. It is used here to convey a comparison or resemblance.</div><div><strong>toi</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It signifies the person to whom the speaker is comparing themselves.</div>','<h2>C’est lui!</h2><div><strong>C’est</strong> This is a contraction of "ce" and "est", which translates to "it is" or "this is" in English. The contraction is often used for statements of identification or existence.</div><div><strong>lui</strong> This is a French pronoun meaning "him" or "he". In this context, it is used to emphasize the person being identified. The sentence "C’est lui!" translates to "It’s him!" or "It is he!" affirming that the person in question is indeed the one being referred to.</div>','<h2>Je travaille avec lui.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>travaille</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "travailler", which means "to work". It indicates the action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This is the French preposition meaning "with". It’s used to describe the company in which the action of the verb is taking place.</div><div><strong>lui</strong> This is a French pronoun meaning "him". In this sentence, it is the indirect object pronoun referring to the person with whom the subject is working.</div>','<h2>Je voudrais être fort comme lui.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>voudrais</strong> This is the conditional tense of the verb "vouloir", which means "to want". It indicates a wish or desire, in this case, "would like".</div><div><strong>être</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It’s used here to express what the speaker would like to be.</div><div><strong>fort</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "strong". Here, it describes what the speaker would like to be, agreeing with the masculine singular subject.</div><div><strong>comme</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "like" or "as". It is used to make a comparison.</div><div><strong>lui</strong> This is a pronoun meaning "him". In this sentence, it’s used to refer to the person the speaker would like to be as strong as.</div>','<h2>Je suis fâché contre lui.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>suis</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". Here, it indicates the current state or condition of the speaker.</div><div><strong>fâché</strong> This is an adjective meaning "angry" or "upset" in French. When used in the masculine singular form, it describes a male subject or an unspecified gender in a formal context. If the subject were female, it would be "fâchée".</div><div><strong>contre</strong> This is a preposition in French which means "against". It is used to indicate opposition or negative feelings towards someone or something.</div><div><strong>lui</strong> This is a pronoun that means "him" in English. In French, "lui" can function as an indirect object (to/at/for him), but in this sentence, it is used after the preposition "contre", thus it functions as a stressed pronoun meaning "against him".</div>','<h2>Je suis meilleure qu’elle au tennis.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>suis</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". In this context, it is used to form the present tense of the verb "être" in a comparative expression involving an adjective.</div><div><strong>meilleure</strong> This is the feminine singular form of the adjective "meilleur", which means "better". It indicates that the subject (the speaker, who is female) is superior in ability or quality at something, in this case, tennis. If the speaker were male, it would be "meilleur".</div><div><strong>qu’elle</strong> This phrase means "than she" or "than her" in English. "Que" is a comparative conjunction meaning "than", and "elle" is a third-person singular pronoun meaning "she" or "her". This part of the sentence is comparing the subject’s skill to that of another female person.</div><div><strong>au tennis</strong> This phrase means "at tennis" in English. "Au" is the contraction of the preposition "à" (meaning "to" or "at") and the definite article "le" (meaning "the"), which together generally mean "at the" when followed by a masculine noun. Here, it is followed by "tennis", which is a masculine noun in French, to indicate the sport being discussed.</div>','<h2>Je fais du vélo avec elle.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>fais</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "faire", which means "to do" or "to make". In this context, it means "do" as part of the expression "faire du vélo", which translates to "to ride a bike".</div><div><strong>du vélo</strong> This is a partitive article "du" combined with the word "vélo", which means "bike". The partitive article is used in French to talk about a portion of something, in this case, some biking activity or the act of riding a bicycle. Essentially it means "bicycle" as in "to ride bicycle".</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This French preposition translates to "with" in English. It is used to indicate accompaniment.</div><div><strong>elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" or "her" in English. It indicates that the action of riding a bike is being done in accompaniment with a female person.</div>','<h2>Je voudrais être aussi courageuse qu’elle.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>voudrais</strong> This is the conditional tense form of the verb "vouloir", which means "to want". In this tense, it translates to "would like", indicating a desire or wish.</div><div><strong>être</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "être", which means "to be". In this sentence, it’s used after the auxiliary verb "voudrais" to express what the speaker would like to be.</div><div><strong>aussi</strong> This is an adverb meaning "as" or "also" in English. It’s used here to compare the extent or degree of a quality.</div><div><strong>courageuse</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "courageous". Here, it’s used in the feminine singular form to agree with the speaker who is implied to be female. If the speaker were male, it would be "courageux".</div><div><strong>qu’</strong> This is a contraction of "que" (which means "than" in comparisons or "that" as a conjunction) and is used here to introduce a comparative phrase. The contraction is necessary because the following word begins with a vowel sound.</div><div><strong>elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It is used here to compare the speaker’s desired level of courage to that of another female individual.</div>','<h2>J’ai dîné avec elle.</h2><div><strong>J’</strong> This is a contraction of "Je", the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It contracts to "J’" because the following word starts with a vowel, making it easier to pronounce.</div><div><strong>ai</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". Here, it’s used as an auxiliary verb to form the past tense, known as the passé composé.</div><div><strong>dîné</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "dîner", which means "to have dinner" or "to dine". When combined with the auxiliary verb "ai", it constructs the passé composé, indicating a completed action in the past.</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This means "with" in English. It’s a preposition that introduces the person who accompanied the speaker during dinner.</div><div><strong>elle</strong> This is the third-person singular feminine pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" or "her" in English. It’s used here to refer to the female person who accompanied the speaker.</div>'];var defBonus = ['<h2>Tu es parti sans moi.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when addressing one person in an informal context. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>es</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". Here, it is used in the auxiliary form in compound past tenses.</div><div><strong>parti</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "partir", which means "to leave" or "to depart". Combined with "es" (from "être"), it forms the passé composé, which is used to express a past action that has been completed. The past participle agrees with the gender and number of the subject; "parti" is the masculine singular form. If the subject were female, it would be "partie".</div><div><strong>sans</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "without". It is used to express the absence of something or someone.</div><div><strong>moi</strong> This is a first-person singular stressed pronoun in French, equivalent to "me" in English. It’s used for emphasis or after prepositions, like "sans" in this sentence.</div>','<h2>Tu es parti après moi.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It’s used here as the subject of the sentence in an informal setting.</div><div><strong>es</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". In this sentence, it is used as an auxiliary verb to form the past tense, known as the passé composé.</div><div><strong>parti</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "partir", which means "to leave". In the passé composé, it is combined with the auxiliary verb "être" to indicate the past action of leaving.</div><div><strong>après</strong> This is a preposition in French that means "after". It indicates the sequence of events in relation to time.</div><div><strong>moi</strong> This is a strong form (tonic pronoun) of the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "me" in English. In this sentence, "moi" is used after a preposition to indicate the object of comparison.</div>','<h2>Dis-moi si ça te plaît.</h2><div><strong>Dis</strong> This is the second-person singular imperative form of the verb "dire", which means "to tell" or "to say". In this sentence, it’s a command telling the listener to say or tell something.</div><div><strong>-moi</strong> This is a pronoun used in French to indicate the indirect object, in this case "me". When attached to a verb like "Dis", it becomes "tell me".</div><div><strong>si</strong> This is a conjunction that translates to "if" in English. It’s used here to introduce a condition.</div><div><strong>ça</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French which means "this" or "that" in English. It’s referring to something previously mentioned or understood in the context.</div><div><strong>te</strong> This is a reflexive or indirect object pronoun that means "to you" in English. It’s indicating whom the action of the sentence is directed towards.</div><div><strong>plaît</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "plaire", which means "to please". It is used here with "te" to form the phrase "it pleases you", which is a way of asking "do you like it?" in English.</div>','<h2>Mon amie paie pour moi.</h2><div><strong>Mon</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French that means "my". It is used here to indicate possession and agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies, which is "amie" (a feminine singular noun).</div><div><strong>amie</strong> This is a French noun for "friend". It is the feminine singular form because it refers to a female friend. If it referred to a male friend, it would be "ami".</div><div><strong>paie</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "payer", which means "to pay". It indicates the action being performed by the subject (the speaker’s friend).</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This is a French preposition that means "for". It is used to indicate the beneficiary or the purpose of the action.</div><div><strong>moi</strong> This is a French pronoun that means "me". It is the object form of "je" and is used here to indicate the person who benefits from the action, which is the person speaking.</div>','<h2>Oui, envoie-moi un texto plus tard.</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It shows agreement or affirmation in response to a statement or question.</div><div><strong>envoie</strong> This is the imperative form of the verb "envoyer", which means "to send". Here, it is used in the second-person singular, telling someone directly to send something.</div><div><strong>moi</strong> This is a French pronoun meaning "me". In this context, it is an indirect object pronoun, meaning that the action of the verb is being performed for or towards "me".</div><div><strong>un texto</strong> This is French informal term for "a text message". The word "texto" is a noun, and "un" is its indefinite article meaning "a".</div><div><strong>plus tard</strong> This phrase translates to "later" in English. "Plus" means "more", and "tard" means "late". Together, they indicate an action should take place at a later time.</div>','<h2>Dites-moi si ça vous fait mal.</h2><div><strong>Dites</strong> This is the imperative form of the French verb "dire", which means "to say" or "to tell". It’s used here to give an order or instruction, addressing you in the plural or formal form.</div><div><strong>-moi</strong> This is a pronoun in the imperative form that means "me". When attached to a verb, it indicates the indirect object to whom the action is directed, in this case, the speaker.</div><div><strong>si</strong> This word translates to "if" in English. It introduces a condition or possibility.</div><div><strong>ça</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun that translates to "that" or "it" in English. It’s used to refer to a previously mentioned or understood object or situation.</div><div><strong>vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It is used here as an indirect object pronoun.</div><div><strong>fait</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "faire", which means "to do" or "to make". Here, it suggests causing something, implying an effect on the person being addressed.</div><div><strong>mal</strong> This word means "pain" or "hurt" in English. Within this expression, "faire mal" means "to cause pain".</div>','<h2>Il écrit mieux que moi.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>écrit</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "écrire", which means "to write". It describes the action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>mieux</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "better". It is used to compare the manner in which the action (in this case, writing) is performed by the subject as opposed to someone else.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a French conjunction or comparative word meaning "than". It is used to set up the comparison between how "he" writes and the performance referred to with "moi".</div><div><strong>moi</strong> This is a French pronoun meaning "me". In this sentence, it is used after the comparative "que" to express that the subject "Il" writes better than "I" do. It is the object of comparison.</div>','<h2>Elle est aussi forte que moi.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence referring to a female individual.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It indicates the condition or quality of the subject being described.</div><div><strong>aussi</strong> This word means "as" or "also" in English. In this context, it is used to indicate an equal degree of comparison between two elements.</div><div><strong>forte</strong> This adjective means "strong" in English. It describes a characteristic of the subject and agrees in gender and number with the feminine subject "elle".</div><div><strong>que</strong> This conjunction means "as" or "than" in English. It is used to introduce the second element of the comparison.</div><div><strong>moi</strong> This is a strong (stressed) pronoun in French, equivalent to "me" in English. It’s used here to refer to the speaker as the second element in the comparison.</div>','<h2>Ils étaient tous là, sauf moi.</h2><div><strong>Ils</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "they" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence, referring to a group of people or things.</div><div><strong>étaient</strong> This is the third-person plural imperfect tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It describes a past state of being or a continuous action in the past that corresponds to "were" in English.</div><div><strong>tous</strong> This word means "all" in English, indicating that the entire group, without exception, was present.</div><div><strong>là</strong> This is an adverb meaning "there" in English. It indicates the location where the group was present.</div><div><strong>sauf</strong> This is a preposition meaning "except" in English. It introduces an exclusion from the previous statement.</div><div><strong>moi</strong> This is a strong or stressed form of the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "me" in English. It is used here to emphasize that the speaker is the only one not included in the group that was present.</div>','<h2>Moi aussi, j’ai passé une très bonne soirée.</h2><div><strong>Moi aussi</strong> This translates to "Me too" in English. "Moi" is a stressed pronoun meaning "me" and "aussi" means "also". Together they are agreeing with a previous statement, indicating that the speaker had a similar experience or feeling.</div><div><strong>j’</strong>This is the contraction of "je", the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It is contracted to "j’" here because the following word starts with a vowel, making it easier to pronounce.</div><div><strong>ai passé</strong> This is the first-person singular past tense form of the verb "passer", which means "to spend" or "to pass". In this context, it means "spent", referring to how the speaker spent their time.</div><div><strong>une</strong> This is a French indefinite article meaning "a" or "an" in English. It comes before feminine singular nouns.</div><div><strong>très</strong> This word translates to "very" in English. It’s an adverb used to intensify the adjective that follows.</div><div><strong>bonne</strong> This is the feminine form of the French adjective "bon", meaning "good". The adjective agrees in gender and number with the noun it describes, which in this case is "soirée" (evening).</div><div><strong>soirée</strong> This is a feminine noun meaning "evening" or "party". Here it indicates the time of day the speaker is referring to. When they say they have spent a "very good evening", it implies enjoyment of the time spent.</div>','<h2>Vous oublieriez tout sans moi.</h2><div><strong>Vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French. In this context, it can mean "you" addressing either one person formally or multiple people. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>oublieriez</strong> This is the conditional tense of the verb "oublier", which means "to forget". It indicates an action that would happen under certain conditions. It is conjugated in the second-person formal or plural to agree with "vous".</div><div><strong>tout</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "all" or "everything". Here, it is the direct object of the verb "oublieriez", indicating what would be forgotten.</div><div><strong>sans</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "without". It is used to describe a condition or circumstance in which something would happen, in this case, the forgetting.</div><div><strong>moi</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "me" in English. It is the object of the preposition "sans" and refers to the person who is speaking or writing.</div>','<h2>Tout le monde est intéressé, sauf moi.</h2><div><strong>Tout le monde</strong> This French phrase translates to "everyone" or "everybody" in English. It is used to refer to all the people in a group.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". In this sentence, it links the subject "tout le monde" to the adjective that follows.</div><div><strong>intéressé</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "interested". Here, it’s used in the masculine singular form to agree with "tout le monde". If referring to a feminine group, it would be "intéressée".</div><div><strong>sauf</strong> This French preposition means "except". It is used to indicate an exclusion from a group or a statement.</div><div><strong>moi</strong> This is the first-person singular stressed pronoun in French, equivalent to "me" in English. It’s used for emphasis and follows prepositions, such as "sauf" in this case.</div>','<h2>Je perds toujours, puisque tu es meilleure que moi.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>perds</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "perdre", which means "to lose". It indicates that the subject is experiencing loss or defeat.</div><div><strong>toujours</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "always". It describes the frequency or consistency of the action denoted by the verb.</div><div><strong>puisque</strong> This is a French conjunction meaning "since" or "because". It is used to introduce a cause or justification for the statement made in the main clause.</div><div><strong>tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when referring to one person in an informal context. It is the subject of the subordinate clause.</div><div><strong>es</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It describes the state of being or identity of the subject in the subordinate clause.</div><div><strong>meilleure</strong> This is the feminine form of the French comparative adjective "meilleur", meaning "better". It signifies that the second person (tu) has a higher level or quality in a certain aspect than the first person (je). The gendered form indicates that the subject referred to as "tu" is female.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This word is a French conjunction meaning "than". It is used in comparative structures to compare two subjects or objects.</div><div><strong>moi</strong> This is a strong (stressed) pronoun in French, equivalent to "me" in English. It is used here to refer back to the subject of the main clause, emphasizing the comparison.</div>','<h2>Je paierais, mais je n’ai plus d’argent sur moi.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It is used as the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>paierais</strong> This is the conditional form of the verb "payer", which means "to pay" in English. The conditional mood is used to express a hypothesis or a possibility, often translated to English as "would pay".</div><div><strong>mais</strong> This word translates to "but" in English. It’s a conjunction used to introduce a clause contrasting with what has been mentioned previously.</div><div><strong>je n’ai</strong> This is a contraction of "je ne ai", translating to "I don’t have" in English. The negation "ne ... plus" means "no longer" or "not ... anymore".</div><div><strong>plus</strong> This is the French word for "more" or "no longer" as part of the negation "ne ... plus". In this sentence, it indicates that the action (having money) is no longer happening.</div><div><strong>d’argent</strong> This is a combination of "de" (of) and "argent" (money), meaning "of money" or simply "money". It is used here to indicate what the subject no longer has.</div><div><strong>sur moi</strong> This phrase means "on me" or "with me" in English. It is used to state that the subject doesn’t have money in their possession.</div>','<h2>Je trouverais une solution, si mon partenaire était avec moi.</h2></div><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>trouverais</strong> This verb is the conditional tense of "trouver", which means "to find" in English. In this case, "trouverais" can be translated as "would find", indicating an action that could happen under a certain condition.</div><div><strong>une</strong> This is the French indefinite article, used here for feminine singular nouns. It translates to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>solution</strong> This is a feminine singular noun in French, meaning "solution" in English. It’s the object of what the subject "would find".</div><div><strong>si</strong> This is a conjunction in French that means "if" in English. It introduces a condition.</div><div><strong>mon</strong> This is the possessive adjective for masculine singular nouns, meaning "my" in English. It is used to indicate possession or a relationship to the subject.</div><div><strong>partenaire</strong> This is a masculine singular noun meaning "partner" in English.</div><div><strong>était</strong> This is the third-person singular imperfect tense of the verb "être", which means "to be" in English. It is used here to indicate a past state or condition that is uncertain or hypothetical.</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This is a preposition meaning "with" in English. It indicates accompaniment or association.</div><div><strong>moi</strong> This is a strong (stressed) pronoun meaning "me" in English. It refers back to the subject "je", emphasizing the speaker themselves.</div>','<h2>Je porterais les chaussures roses, mais elles sont trop grandes pour moi.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>porterais</strong> This is the conditional form of the verb "porter", which means "to wear" or "to carry". It suggests an action that could happen under certain conditions or in hypothetical situations.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is the definite article in French for plural nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It precedes the noun it modifies.</div><div><strong>chaussures</strong> This is a French noun meaning "shoes". It is plural and feminine, as indicated by the article "les".</div><div><strong>roses</strong> This is an adjective meaning "pink" in English. It agrees in gender and number with the noun it describes, which is "chaussures" (shoes), hence it is in the feminine plural form.</div><div><strong>mais</strong> This word translates to "but" in English. It introduces a contrasting statement or clause.</div><div><strong>elles</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun, equivalent to "they" in English. It refers back to "les chaussures" (the shoes).</div><div><strong>sont</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be".</div><div><strong>trop</strong> This adverb translates to "too" in English. It indicates an excessive degree of some quality.</div><div><strong>grandes</strong> This is the feminine plural form of the adjective "grand", which means "big" or "large". It agrees with the noun "chaussures" (shoes) in gender and number.</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This preposition translates as "for" in English. It is used to indicate the beneficiary of an action or the intended purpose of something.</div><div><strong>moi</strong> This is a disjunctive pronoun meaning "me" in English. It is used here to indicate who the shoes are too large for.</div>','<h2>Je suis devant toi.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>suis</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". In this context, it denotes location or position.</div><div><strong>devant</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "in front of". When used with "être" as in "être devant", it describes someone’s position relative to another.</div><div><strong>toi</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It’s used here to indicate the person who is being spoken to.</div>','<h2>Je suis d’accord avec toi.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>suis</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". In this context, it is part of the expression "être d’accord" which means "to agree".</div><div><strong>d’accord</strong> This is a French phrase that means "in agreement" or "agree". It’s part of the expression "être d’accord" which means "to agree".</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This is the French preposition for "with". It’s used to indicate the relationship between "je" (I) and "toi" (you).</div><div><strong>toi</strong> This is the second-person singular stressed pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when used after a preposition or for emphasis.</div>','<h2>Il veut partir avec toi.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>veut</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "vouloir", which means "to want". It describes the desire of the subject, "he wants".</div><div><strong>partir</strong> This is an infinitive verb in French that means "to leave" or "to depart". In this sentence, it’s used to describe the action the subject wants to take.</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "with". It is used to indicate accompaniment or association.</div><div><strong>toi</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in the informal tone in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It indicates the person that the subject wants to leave with.</div>','<h2>Bien sûr, je me souviens de toi!</h2><div><strong>Bien sûr</strong> This phrase means "of course" in English. It is used to express agreement or to confirm something without any doubt.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>me</strong> This is a reflexive pronoun in the first-person singular. In French, it is used to indicate that the subject is performing an action upon itself, which in English often corresponds to the word "myself".</div><div><strong>souviens</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "se souvenir", which means "to remember". It is used here in its reflexive form to express that the speaker is remembering.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This preposition is often translated to English as "of" or "from" and can indicate origin, possession, or the object of remembrance, among other relations.</div><div><strong>toi</strong> This word is the informal second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when referring to one person in an informal setting or when talking to a friend.</div>','<h2>Oui. J’ai aimé passer du temps avec toi.</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It affirms a statement or responds affirmatively to a question.</div><div><strong>J’</strong> This is a contraction of "Je" before a vowel or mute h, still meaning "I" in English. It’s used here as the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>ai</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the auxiliary verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this context, it is used to form the past tense known as "passé composé".</div><div><strong>aimé</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "aimer", which means "to like" or "to love". In the "passé composé" tense, it combines with the auxiliary verb "avoir" to indicate a completed action, so "ai aimé" translates to "liked" or "have liked".</div><div><strong>passer</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "passer", which means "to spend" or "to pass". It’s used in the infinitive form as it follows the conjugated verb "ai aimé".</div><div><strong>du</strong> This is a contraction of the preposition "de" and the definite article "le", which together can mean "of the" or "from the". In the context of time, "du temps" translates to "some time" or "a period of time".</div><div><strong>temps</strong> This is a French noun meaning "time". It’s the object of the verb phrase "passer du temps", which together means "to spend time".</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This is the French preposition meaning "with". It indicates the company in which an action is performed.</div><div><strong>toi</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when addressing one person informally. It’s used here to indicate the person with whom the speaker spent time.</div>','<h2>Je vais penser à toi pendant mes vacances.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It is used as the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>vais</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "aller", which means "to go" in English. In this sentence, it is used to form the near future tense.</div><div><strong>penser</strong> This is an infinitive verb in French that means "to think". In this sentence, it suggests an action that the subject will do in the future.</div><div><strong>à</strong> This is a preposition that can have several meanings such as "to", "at", or "in". Here, it is used with the verb "penser" to mean "thinking of" or "about".</div><div><strong>toi</strong> This is the stressed pronoun for "you" in French, used here to indicate the direct object or the entity that is being thought about.</div><div><strong>pendant</strong> This is a preposition meaning "during" in English. It signifies a time frame in which the action will take place.</div><div><strong>mes</strong> This possessive adjective means "my" in English, denoting ownership or relation to the subject. It is in its plural form because it is relating to the plural noun "vacances".</div><div><strong>vacances</strong> This is a noun meaning "vacation" or "holidays" in English, and it is in the plural form.</div>','<h2>Je lirais avec toi, mais ce livre est ennuyant.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular subject pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English.</div><div><strong>lirais</strong> This is the conditional tense of the verb "lire", which means "to read" in English. It expresses a hypothetical or polite offer in this sentence, corresponding to "would read" in English.</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This French preposition means "with" in English. It indicates the company of another person in the action.</div><div><strong>toi</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It is used here to indicate the person to whom the offer of reading is made.</div><div><strong>mais</strong> This word translates to "but" in English. It’s a conjunction used to introduce a contrasting clause or statement.</div><div><strong>ce</strong> This is the demonstrative adjective in French meaning "this" in English. It’s used here to specify which book is being described.</div><div><strong>livre</strong> This is a French noun meaning "book" in English. It’s the object being talked about.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", meaning "to be" in English. It links the subject "ce livre" to its descriptor.</div><div><strong>ennuyant</strong> This is a present participle used as an adjective in French, translating to "boring" or "tedious" in English. It describes the book that is the subject of the sentence.</div>','<h2>Je danserais avec toi toute la journée si je le pouvais.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the French first-person singular subject pronoun, equivalent to "I" in English. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>danserais</strong> This is the first-person singular conditional tense of the verb "danser", which means "to dance". This tense is used to express a potential action that could happen under certain conditions.</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "with". It’s used here to indicate the company in which the action of dancing is performed.</div><div><strong>toi</strong> This is the French second-person singular pronoun, meaning "you" when addressing one person in an informal setting.</div><div><strong>toute la journée</strong> This phrase means "all day long". "Toute" is an adjective meaning "whole" or "entire", "la" is the definite article "the", and "journée" means "day".</div><div><strong>si</strong> This is a conjunction meaning "if". It introduces a conditional clause, indicating the condition under which the action of the main clause (dancing all day) would take place.</div><div><strong>je</strong> Again, this is the French first-person singular pronoun, meaning "I". In this part of the sentence, it is used to refer again to the subject as part of the conditional clause.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the direct object pronoun "it", referring back to the potential action or situation discussed earlier (i.e., dancing all day).</div><div><strong>pouvais</strong> This is the first-person singular imperfect tense of the verb "pouvoir", which means "to be able to". It is used here conditionally to express the ability to perform the action in the past or under certain conditions that are not met.</div>','<h2>Tu penses toujours à lui.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence, used for informal situations when addressing a person directly.</div><div><strong>penses</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "penser", which means "to think". It indicates the action being performed by the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>toujours</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "always". It indicates that the action of thinking is continuous or habitual.</div><div><strong>à</strong> This is a French preposition that can have several meanings, such as "to", "at", or "in". In this context, it is used to express the direction of the thoughts toward someone, meaning "about" or "of".</div><div><strong>lui</strong> This is a French pronoun referring to a third-person singular male, equivalent to "him" in English. It is the object of the preposition "à", indicating the person who is being thought of.</div>','<h2>Je me couche plus tard que lui.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>me</strong> This is a reflexive pronoun that refers back to the subject. In this case, it is part of the reflexive verb "se coucher", which means "to go to bed".</div><div><strong>couche</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the reflexive verb "se coucher", which means "to go to bed" or "to lie down".</div><div><strong>plus</strong> This is an adverb meaning "more". In this context, it is used for comparison and means "later" as part of the phrase "plus tard".</div><div><strong>tard</strong> This is an adverb meaning "late". When combined with "plus" (more), it becomes "later".</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a conjunction meaning "than" and is used in comparisons.</div><div><strong>lui</strong> This is a pronoun meaning "him". It functions as the object of comparison in the sentence, indicating that "I go to bed later than he does".</div>','<h2>Je me réveillais toujours avant lui.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>me</strong> This is a reflexive pronoun in French. It’s used with reflexive verbs and indicates that the subject is performing the action on themselves. In this sentence, it’s part of "se réveiller", which means "to wake up".</div><div><strong>réveillais</strong> This is the first-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "se réveiller", which means "to wake up". The imperfect tense is used to describe ongoing or repeated past actions. Here, it suggests that "I used to wake up" or "I was waking up" regularly at a time before someone else.</div><div><strong>toujours</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "always". It indicates that the action of waking up was a habitual event.</div><div><strong>avant</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "before". It indicates that the action of waking up occurred prior to the time the other person woke up.</div><div><strong>lui</strong> This is a stressed pronoun in French, which corresponds to "him" or "her" in English. In this sentence, "lui" refers to the person the subject is waking up before. It is a masculine pronoun, so it indicates "him" in this context.</div>','<h2>Je m’assoirais pour parler avec lui tous les matins, si je le pouvais.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>m’assoirais</strong> This is the first-person singular conditional tense form of the reflexive verb "s’asseoir", which means "to sit down". In this case, "m’assoirais" is "I would sit down". The conditional tense is used to express an action that would happen under certain conditions.</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This is a preposition in French which means "for" in English. It introduces a purpose or a reason for the action.</div><div><strong>parler</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "parler", which means "to speak" or "to talk" in English. Here, it describes the action that would be performed when sitting down.</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This is a preposition in French which means "with" in English. It indicates the companionship in the action of speaking.</div><div><strong>lui</strong> This is a third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "him" in English. It’s the object of the preposition "avec" and indicates the person with whom the speaker would like to talk.</div><div><strong>tous les matins</strong> This phrase means "every morning" in English. "Tous" means "every" or "all", "les" is the definite article for plural nouns, and "matins" is the plural of "matin", which means "morning".</div><div><strong>si</strong> This word translates to "if" in English. It’s a conjunction used to introduce a condition.</div><div><strong>je</strong> Again, this is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s used here to refer to the subject of the conditional clause.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the direct object pronoun in French, equivalent to "it" in English when referring to a masculine noun or action. It’s referring back to the act of sitting down and talking.</div><div><strong>pouvais</strong> This is the first-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "pouvoir", which means "to be able to" or "can" in English. The imperfect tense is used to describe a state or a habitual action in the past and is used here to indicate a hypothetical ability in the conditional clause.</div>','<h2>Il ouvrira la portière de la voiture pour elle.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>ouvrira</strong> This is the third-person singular future tense form of the verb "ouvrir", which means "to open". In this sentence, it indicates a future action that "he" will perform.</div><div><strong>la portière</strong> This is a feminine singular noun that means "the car door" in English. In French, nouns have genders, and the article "la" is used for feminine singular nouns.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition in French that can mean "of" or "from". In this context, it is used to indicate the object associated with the verb.</div><div><strong>la voiture</strong> This is a feminine singular noun phrase which means "the car". It is accompanied by the feminine article "la" to agree with the gender and number of the noun.</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This is a preposition that means "for" in English. It indicates the beneficiary or the purpose of the action.</div><div><strong>elle</strong> This is the third-person singular feminine pronoun in French, equivalent to "her" in English. It indicates the person for whom the action of opening the car door will be done.</div>'];definitions = definitions.concat(defBonus);